<Project TreatAsLocalProperty="TreatWarningsAsErrors">

  <!--
    MARS targets net10.0.

    It used to build net8.0 by default with net10.0 behind an opt-in property, because
    pwiz-sharp - the ProteoWizard .NET port MARS reads vendor formats through - was itself
    net8.0, and .NET reference compatibility is forward-only: a net8.0 project cannot
    reference a net10.0 one. pwiz-sharp retargeted to net10.0 in ProteoWizard/pwiz PR #4619,
    so the constraint now points the other way and MARS has to be net10.0 to build against it
    at all.

    That raises the SDK floor: building MARS needs a .NET 10 SDK (10.0.100 or newer, which is
    what pwiz's own global.json asks for). RUNNING a release artifact needs nothing - they are
    self-contained - and a framework-dependent build rolls forward, so this is a build-machine
    requirement rather than a deployment one.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <!-- Pinned rather than 'latest' so the language version does not silently track
         whichever SDK a developer happens to have installed. C# 14 is the ceiling of the
         .NET 10 SDK, so this is the newest version CI can compile; naming it means a
         developer on a future SDK cannot use syntax that compiles locally and fails in CI.
         Raising this means raising the SDK floor in .github/workflows/dotnet.yml too. -->
    <LangVersion>14</LangVersion>
    <!-- Both of these were inherited defaults under the .NET 8 SDK and are named here only
         because moving to SDK 10 changed them underneath us.

         NuGetAuditMode: .NET 9 changed the default from "direct" to "all", so restore began
         auditing the transitive graph. Under -warnaserror - which the vendor job passes to a
         build that restores - an advisory published against anything below Parquet.Net would
         fail CI with nothing in MARS changed. "direct" is the pre-existing behaviour and what
         pwiz-sharp pins for itself. Transitive advisories are still findable on demand with
         `dotnet list package` with the vulnerable and include-transitive switches, which is
         how the Snappier one in MARS.IO was caught. (Those switches are spelled out rather
         than written literally because a double hyphen cannot appear inside an XML comment.)

         AnalysisLevel: pinned for the same reason LangVersion is. It otherwise tracks the
         SDK, so a future 10.0.x feature band could introduce an analyzer warning that
         -warnaserror turns into a build failure without a commit to MARS. -->
    <NuGetAuditMode>direct</NuGetAuditMode>
    <!-- Set here, and fenced by the TreatAsLocalProperty on the Project element above, so
         that the vendor builds can pass -p:TreatWarningsAsErrors=false for pwiz's projects
         without taking MARS's own code with it.

         Why they pass it: MARS compiles pwiz-sharp from source, from a tree cloned at the
         commit dotnet/pwiz-sharp.json names, and pwiz sets TreatWarningsAsErrors for its own
         projects. Anything pwiz warns about is therefore a build failure here, in code this
         repository cannot change without moving the pin. A restore-time NuGet advisory is
         the case that bites: pwiz pins System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 8.0.4 as the clean
         version, four advisories were published against it on 2026-10-13, and the vendor job
         and the release publish both began failing with nothing in MARS changed.

         Turning pwiz's promotion off rather than the audit is the point, and it lets nothing
         through to the binary. pwiz's audit still runs and still prints every advisory against
         what pwiz declares. What MARS ships is checked separately and more strictly:
         MARS.Pwiz audits its WHOLE package graph when pwiz is present, so an advisory against
         any version that actually reaches MARS - whoever declared it - fails the build,
         because this property stays true for everything under dotnet/ and NuGet reads it
         directly. MARS.Pwiz also overrides that pin with a patched 10.0.x release, which is
         why the build passes today: pwiz's 8.0.4 is declared, not shipped.

         A -p: value is a global property and would otherwise override what a project sets.
         TreatAsLocalProperty demotes it to a local one for this directory, which lets the
         value here win. The CI job passes -warnnotaserror:NU1903 as well, because its
         -warnaserror is an MSBuild-level promotion that ignores this property; MARS projects
         are still covered there by the value set here. Verified by restoring the vendor
         build with both flags and a deliberately vulnerable direct reference in MARS.Test:
         MARS.Test failed, pwiz's Sciex.csproj reported its four advisories as warnings. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <AnalysisLevel>10.0</AnalysisLevel>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <Company>University of Washington</Company>
    <Product>MARS</Product>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) University of Washington 2026</Copyright>
    <Version>26.5.0</Version>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
